  "query_preview": "..Despite PWATs dipping across the area this\nafternoon, ample moisture remains in the lower levels with \nsufficient dynamics for afternoon convection in place. Storm \ncoverage will be more isolated in nature than past days, and \nmainly favoring southern Utah and far eastern Utah. Upper level \nflow remains lethargic, allowing storms that form along terrain to\ndrift slowly and produce locally heavy rainfall and potentially \nimpactful short-term accumulations.\n\nThis drier airmass will be short lived as a shortwave trough \nquickly ejects into the Central Plains Friday and shifts the ridge\naxis back into a better position to drive moisture well into \nnorthern parts of Utah and southwestern Wyoming. With that, \nprobabilities increase for more widespread showers and\nthunderstorms, though the highest chances both for initiation and\nprecipitation intensity are over central and southern Utah. As\nsuch, these areas are being highlighted for a heightened flash \nflood risk, with the collaborated Excessive Rainfall Outlook (ERO) \nincorporating areas generally east of I-15 and south of I-70 into \na Marginal Risk (level 1/4) for excessive rainfall. The axis of\nthis broad ridge gradually shifts back eastward through the \nweekend into early next week, resulting in continued monsoonal \nmoisture transport through this period that will bring continued \nchances for afternoon showers and thunderstorms capable of \nproducing flash flooding each day.\n\nIn addition to a return to wetter conditions, this pattern\nwill bring an increase in temperatures especially across northern\nUtah Friday into the weekend as the ridge not only shifts but\nH5 heights continue to climb as the ridge amplifies. Daytime \nhighs soar back to the century mark for many locations across the \nWasatch Front, with overnight lows in the mid to upper 70s \noffering very little relief. As such, widespread Major HeatRisk \nemerges through this period, indicating an elevated threat for \nheat illness especially for those without adequate cooling and \nhydration. Areas where cloud cover and remnant surface moisture\nwill see the least overnight cooling, and there is indication that\nsome areas with lows sitting around 80F could reach Extreme \nHeatRisk (~20% chance). If outdoors for festivities this weekend,\nmake sure to stay hydrated and take breaks in the shade or air \nconditioning often.",
